{
  "id": "funnel_p_physician_power",
  "reference": "Detectability and per-point intervals on a physician-scale p funnel. Fourteen surgeons, 12 to 260 cases, pooled complication rate 11.6%. The surgeon sorted first (4 of 12, 33%) does NOT flag at nearly three times the center line: min_detectable = 0.6559 says an 80%-power flag there needs a true rate of 66%, and the Clopper-Pearson 95% CI (0.0992, 0.6511) spans 'better than average' to 'over half'. The largest surgeon flags at 18.5% because 260 cases buy the resolution. Twelve of fourteen points cannot detect even a doubling of the center line (min_detectable / cl > 2), so summary.underpowered is true and power_note is composed. Detectability uses the chart's own signal event (strictly y > ucl, so X >= floor(ucl*n)+1) and exact binomial inversion in theta; both it and the CI were cross-checked against direct math.comb sums: power 0.8 and tail masses 0.025 to ~1e-15. Upper contours sit at the exact one-sided test's boundary, one count beyond the interpolated quantile, so a point is beyond either line exactly when its exact p-value is below that line's tail probability (docs/methods.md \u00a74.1).",
